Three sampling sites for soil were selected. 1kg of sample was
collected at each sampling site and the control site at a depth of
18cm each using a spade. The samples were taken from 50,
100 and 150 m away from production site and then labeled as
samples A, B & C. The soil samples were grounded using
pestle and mortar, dried and sieved through 2 mm size. They
were then taken to Faculty of Agriculture Laboratory, Bayero
University Kano for heavy metal content analysis.
Water samples
Two samples of water labeled D and E were collected in 75cl
bottles from boreholes 50 m away from the project site and
then taken to Faculty of Agriculture Laboratory, Bayero
University Kano for heavy metal content analysis.
Determination of heavy metal in the soil samples
One gramme(1g) of dried and sieved soil sample form site A,
site B, site C and control site was collected at each site. The
samples and a blank were digested with 5ml of concentrated
nitric acid (HNO3) and 2 ml of 70% Perchloric acid (HCIO4).
Each beaker was covered with a watch glass and the digestion
was carried out on a hot plate in a fume chamber until it’s
colour turned white. The digestion continued until the entire
volume was reduced to about 15 ml. The digestion was allowed
to cool to room temperature and then filtered. The mixture was
diluted with 5ml of 20% Nitric acid. Atomic absorption
spectrophotometer Model No 240FSAA was used to analyze
the samples extract (ASTM, 1981).
Determination of heavy metals in the water samples
50 ml of each sample was acidified with 20 ml of nitric,
hydrochloric and perchloric acid. The mixture was digested on
a hot plate in a fume chamber for one hour at 100oC until clear
solution was seen and the volume reduces to 20 ml with
presence of white fumes. The mixture was filtered with filter
paper after cooling and transferred to100 ml plastic bottle.

Deionized water was added into the bottle up to 50 ml mark.
Atomic Absorption Spectrophotometer (AAS) was used to
analyze samples (Dauda, 2011).
Quantification of Heavy Metal Contamination/Pollution
Index
The level of contamination in the soil samples was determined
using the quantification of contamination/pollution index
(MPI) of metals in soil adopted by Lacatusu (2000).
